✅ **AP-15 SLICE 1 SHIPPED s1653 `909c0d7c` (drained: tsc+build+own guard+test:node-guards rc=0 293.0s, --check bite proven by manufactured defect; non-blocking F-1653-3 on eraStamp; review reviews/ap15-1-null-floor-anchors.md). AUTHORED s1650 (FIRE-AUTHORED — attended review welcome) — `tasks/lane-a-ap15-1-null-floor-anchors.md` → lane-a; goal leaf `ap15-1-null-floor-anchors` registered in the same commit.** THE NULL FLOOR: pinned idle-run anchors that measure **what the MAP gives away free**, from `specs/agent-play/ap-15-assay-of-minds.md`'s **"What ships first (cheap, in order)"** item 1. It is the artifact the spec's own Goodhart clause (law 2, *"decision economy is void where the null floor secures"*) depends on — until it exists, law 2 is unenforceable and every efficiency number the Field Book prints is unanchored. 💰 **PRICED BEFORE AUTHORING, WHICH IS THE POINT — F-1648-1 was parked one fire ago precisely because a full-corpus run reached the queue unmeasured (~9h):** measured on main `784e94f02`, sequential, **63.2 s for 17 contracts × 1 seed** (range 1.4–8.4 s), so the full corpus of **35 seeds ≈ 2.5 minutes**. ✅ **Three premises proved before a word of scope was written:** (1) **exactly 12 of the 17 bench contracts are door-servable** — which matches the spec's *"the 12 door-servable contracts"* exactly, so the spec was precise and it is `bench-seeds.json` that grew past the door; the other 5 (`e3-fairground` + the four e6) are refused by `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` and are **already documented, not a bug** (F-DOOR-4, 2026-08-08, cured by the `door-contracts` block in `public/skill.md`) — explicitly firewalled OUT of the master; (2) **determinism verified** by running `the-claim`, `e1-baron` and `e2-hill-mine` twice each — `eventLogHash`, `waves`, `kills` **and** `timeMs` reproduced identically, which is what makes a pinned artifact and a `--check` drift mode meaningful at all; (3) the derivation must **not** be wired into any standing battery (only a millisecond shape-guard is rooted, in `test:node-guards`) — a 2.5-minute sim corpus inside a gate is the exact shape that produced F-1648-1. 📊 **Two results already banked from the pricing runs, worth keeping whatever the master returns:** **no servable contract idle-SECURES at its first seed**, so F-BAL-1's zero-decision win does not reproduce anywhere in the current servable set (a negative result, stated as one); and the free ride varies enormously by map — **`e2-hill-mine` and `e2-trestle` survive 18 idle waves** against `e2-pressure-garden`'s **1**, which is exactly the spread the anchor exists to expose. ⚠️ **ONE STATED REFINEMENT, FLAGGED FOR ATTENDED VETO:** the master derives **35** per-(contract,seed) floors where the spec's slice-1 text says *"12 idle runs"* — county bench rows are keyed per seed, so a per-contract floor could not be compared against the row it anchors; the spec's "12" reads as an effort estimate, not a grain ruling, and the finer grain costs ~2.5 min. Reversing it is a one-line filter over the same file.

🟡 **F-1649-1 (s1649 2026-08-11, MEASURED WHILE DECIDING WHAT TO AUTHOR INTO FOUR IDLE LANES — **AP-16-6 IS NOT FIRE-AUTHORABLE, AND THE REASON IS THAT EVERY ONE OF ITS "OWED" VERBS HAS NO HEADLESS SYSTEM TO ATTACH TO.** ATTENDED-OWED, NOT OWNER-GATED — the same shape as F-1641-3, filed so the next fire does not spend its budget re-deriving it.)** With `ap16-5b` merged, AP-16-6 is the last AP-16 rung and every recent handoff names it as authorable — s1646's (A) called it *"genuinely authorable"* and s1645's (B) *"the last AP-16 rung"*, both with the sound instruction to **scope it to ONE verb per master**. I went to do exactly that and the premise did not survive the first probe. ✗ **The spec slice (`specs/agent-play/ap-16-same-game-law.md:35`) classifies five verbs as OWED — `TOGGLE_WEAPON` · `RESEARCH_PICK` · `SECURE_CHOICE` · `DEATH_ACTION` · `CONTEXT_ACTION` — and closes with *"Each verb: same rules, same costs, additive hashes."* **That closing promise is measurably false for all five**, because the systems they would drive do not exist headless.** 🔬 **MEASURED, file by file, not inferred from the audit table:** **(1) `TOGGLE_WEAPON`** — `weaponToggle` is consumed in exactly ONE place in the whole tree, `src/game/Game.ts:2473` (`if (intents.weaponToggle) this.toggleWeapon()`), which is the BROWSER game. In the sim it is an **inert struct member**: `src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts:186` initialises `weaponToggle: false` and **nothing ever reads it**, while the outcome ledger at `:972` reports `weaponToggles: 0` as a **hardcoded literal**. There is no headless weapon state to toggle. **(2) `RESEARCH_PICK` / `SECURE_CHOICE` / `DEATH_ACTION`** — `grep` for `research`, `secureChoice`, `deathAction` and `secure_choice` over `src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts` returns **zero hits each**, and the sim's 36 imports compose no research, secure-choice or death-action system (the single `esearch` hit anywhere under `src/sim/` is `E9ArsenalSocket.ts`, an unrelated arsenal surface). ⚖️ **WHY THAT MAKES IT NON-AUTHORABLE RATHER THAN MERELY BIGGER:** adding any of these verbs means **porting a system into the sim**, which changes what the sim DOES — hero attack resolution for the weapon, run-end agency for secure/death — and therefore **moves event-log hashes**, contradicting the slice's own additive promise and every "additive, default preserves every existing hash" assertion AP-16-4/5 landed on. That is a design decision about sim behaviour, which `fire.md` §2E's HARD LIMIT reserves (*"if the lane's next work has no spec slice, needs a design fork, or bends canon, do NOT author"*). The slice EXISTS, so this is not the F-1641-3 missing-slice case exactly — it is the subtler one: **a slice whose stated premise its own implementation surface refutes.** 🔎 **AND A SHARPER QUESTION FELL OUT OF THE SAME PROBE, WHICH IS THE HALF WORTH THE ATTENDED SESSION'S MINUTE:** the audit files `weapon_toggle` as **agent-lacks on all 42 contracts** (`docs/bench/same-game-audit.md:90` and 41 siblings), but AP-16-3 already gave agents `BLAST_AT` — a **direct** verb reaching the blast pool at `HeadlessContractSim.ts:1305`. So the two species now reach the blast charge by **different mechanisms**: the human toggles the active weapon (`rig` ↔ `blast`, `src/ui/Hud.ts:260`) and presumably surrenders Spark Rig output while holding it, whereas the agent calls `BLAST_AT` and **keeps firing the rig**. ⚠️ **If that reading is right, the agent does not LACK the toggle — it EXCEEDS the human by never paying the toggle's opportunity cost**, which is the same-game law's rule 3 violated in the direction the audit is not looking. I did **not** verify the opportunity cost (it lives in `Game.ts`'s attack path, outside what this probe read), so this is stated as the question it is, not as a finding. **GATE: ATTENDED — re-slice AP-16-6 against its real implementation surface.** The three live options, none of which a fire may pick: **(a)** port the systems and accept an era re-stamp for the hash movement; **(b)** rule the five verbs EXEMPT-with-cited-reason as browser-input artifacts with no headless referent, which is the *"exemption is a debt, not a policy"* path AP-16-4 already established; **(c)** decide the `BLAST_AT`-vs-toggle equivalence question above first, since a ruling that agents already have ability parity may retire `TOGGLE_WEAPON` outright. ⓘ **No goal leaf exists for AP-16-6** (`drain-block-check ap16-6` → UNKNOWN, and a `goals.json` walk for `ap16-6` returns `[]`), so nothing on the board is left stale by not authoring it.

🔵 **F-1648-2 (s1648 2026-08-11, CURED IN THE SAME FIRE — THE FACTORY REPORTED `RESUMED` WHILE ITS IMPLEMENT SIDE WAS DEAD, AND IT STAYED DEAD BECAUSE TWO SEPARATE `pgrep -f` CHECKS EACH MATCHED THE COMMAND THAT WAS RUNNING THEM).** The resume one-liner was `until ! pgrep -f "FIRE PROTOCOL"; do sleep 15; done; nohup bash scripts/lane-runner-v3.sh …`, run as one backgrounded shell. **Both of its guards are self-matching, because the whole command line — including its own search strings — is the argv `pgrep -f` searches:** (a) the wait loop matches its own shell forever, so the restart it guards **can never fire**; (b) `lane-runner-v3.sh`'s otherwise-correct stale-lock self-heal — *"if no OTHER runner process exists, the lock is a corpse"* — counted that same wrapper as another runner, declined to clear the corpse lock left by the killed loop, and logged *"another instance running … Remove if stale."* **Net effect: the ops commit says the runner was restarted, `tasks/.runner.lock` was a month-old corpse, and no runner process existed at all.** ⚠️ **The dangerous part is not the outage, it is that the LEDGER SAID OTHERWISE** — Mistake #4's shape at factory scale: a fire reading the resume commit inherits *"runners restarted"* and never probes. ✅ **CURED s1648**: corpse lock cleared, runner started detached with `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` **scrubbed from its environment** (a runner started from a fire shell would otherwise inherit the fire's config dir and silently pin every LANE playwright run to `workers: 1` via the `isFireShell` key in `playwright.config.ts` — §3.1's mechanism firing in exactly the direction it is written to forbid), and verified up by pidfile+log rather than by the fact that the command returned. 💡 **GENERAL: `pgrep -f <string>` run from a shell whose own command line contains `<string>` always finds itself. Any liveness probe written inline in the command it is probing for is a tautology** — probe from a script file, or match a pattern the probing command cannot contain.

🔵 **F-1648-3 (s1648 2026-08-11, RESTORED IN THE SAME FIRE — A FIRE KILLED MID-CYCLE TAKES ITS PREDECESSOR'S HANDOFF WITH IT, BECAUSE THE ARCHIVE DUTY IS DISCHARGED AT HANDOFF TIME AND A KILLED FIRE NEVER GETS THERE).** s1647's lock commit replaced line-1 without archiving s1646's handoff — the **normal** pattern, which `status-archive-audit` scores as normal precisely because the fire's own handoff commit restores it. Then the owner pause killed s1647 mid-cycle (no `FIRE END` in the fire log), so the restoring commit was never written and **s1646's entire handoff — one drain, its gates, and two explicitly OWED duties — sat in no bullet at all**, recoverable only from git. ⚠️ **The §4 check that catches this is already written** (*"`grep -c "s<N-1> handoff (line-1 archive)"` and expect 1"*) **but it is prescribed as a pre-commit check for the fire writing the handoff, so no one runs it on a fire that never handed off.** ✅ Recovered verbatim from the s1646 STATUS commit and archived s1648. 💡 **GENERAL: the drop-then-restore pattern is safe only if the restoring fire survives. Any fire taking over a lock it did not set should `grep -c` for its predecessor's predecessor before assuming the chain is intact** — the cost is one command and the loss is otherwise silent.

🔵 **F-1646-1 (s1646 2026-08-11, advisory — NOTHING OWED, recorded so the next reader knows the guard's denominator): the `ap16-5b` rotation test pins its rejection ruling at `7` and `'north'` only, which is narrower than the ruling it defends.** `scripts/agent-seat.test.mjs`'s new case — *"the seat carries the facing, and refuses a facing the county does not keep"* — asserts the two values the master named, which covers the **type** axis (`'north'`) and the **upper bound** (`7`) but leaves the lower bound (`-1`), the non-integer case (`1.5`) and `null` unasserted. ✅ **All three were verified correct at the s1646 gate** by driving `SeatOrdersDriver.submit()` directly (each returns `INVALID_ARGS`; table in `reviews/ap16-5b-seat-rotation-parity.md` and `artifacts/s1646-ap16-5b-teeth-probe.md`), so **there is no defect and nothing is broken** — the shipped code rejects all five. This is a note about *test coverage*, not behaviour: widening the case to the full set is a one-line change worth doing opportunistically the next time that file is open, and is explicitly **not** worth a queue slot. ⓘ Filed because a ruling asserted at two points is asserted at two points, not across a range, and the next person to change `parseSeatOrder` deserves to know which arms are actually held down.

🔵 **F-1646-2 (s1646 2026-08-11 — CURED IN THE SAME FIRE, filed for the class not the instance): a GHOST LADDER ROW sat `planned` while the work it names had shipped in full, and the guard built for ghost rows could not see it.** `tasks/goals.json`'s `ap16-5-build-rotation` — *"AP-16-5: BUILD gains rotationSteps parity (audit class 7)"* — was the **ladder-plan** row for AP-16-5, and it was still `planned` after the work landed under **two** implementation leaves: `ap16-5-rotation-parity` (`144b3291`, the standing-orders door) and `ap16-5b-seat-rotation-parity` (`57208ef8`, the seat). Retired s1646 to `superseded` + `supersededBy: ap16-5-rotation-parity`, with the pointer **WALKED, not assumed** (the titles and hashes were read and matched before the flip). ⚠️ **Why it is worth a row even though it is fixed: left alone it is a Mistake #8 magnet** — a fire reading the ladder sees an OPEN rung for fully-shipped work and can queue a master against it, which is the 824k-token flail shape, and memory records this pattern hitting **7 of 10** sampled ladder rows. 🔎 **The instructive half: `scripts/ghost-ladder-row-guard.mjs --strict` reported `0 ghost ladder row(s)` on this exact tree, before and after.** The leaf carries **no `taskFile` and no BACKLOG row of its own** — a bare plan row — and that shape is outside the guard's denominator. **Deliberately NOT cured by a second mechanism** (a rival resolver duplicating the first is the sprawl the existing guards' headers warn against); recorded instead so the next reader knows a green from that guard does not mean "no ghost rows", it means "no ghost rows *of the kind it counts*".

🔵 **F-1646-3 (s1646 2026-08-11, MEASURED while authoring `f1643-2` — a GATE STATED IN THE WRONG UNITS: F-1643-2's quiet-machine gate is written in terms of LANES, and lanes are not the only load on this machine).** The gate reads *"author this only when all four lanes are idle and no drain is in flight"*, and its purpose is a machine quiet enough that a full-suite snapshot measures the **code** rather than the **box** (F-1270-1's load ceiling). At s1646 the letter was satisfied — all four lanes `ahead=0` USABLE, the fire's drain committed — **and the box was not quiet:** `ps` showed **Blender at 100% CPU for 2h26m** (`Blender -b assets/blender/fries-mcdx/landmark.blend`, a headless art-pipeline render) **plus `fseventsd` at 100%**, i.e. **2 of 16 physical cores saturated by work no lane instrument can see.** ⚠️ **A fire checking only `lane-usable --all` would have called the machine quiet and been wrong** — and would then have banked a load-polluted snapshot as a fresh set of *excused* reds, the outcome the RE-PRICED row calls *"strictly worse than the 13-day-old one it replaced."* ✅ **The gate's own third clause already handles this** (*"state the concurrent load it ran under"*), which is why the cure is to OBEY it rather than to rewrite it: the dispatched master records load at run start **and** end, and its scope 5 re-runs every failing spec file **serially, once**, so anything that fails parallel-but-passes-serial is declared **load-attributable** instead of entering the inventory. 🪞 **And the same trap caught its own author, which is the part worth reading:** s1646 dispatched that full-suite measurement and then started `test:ledger-guards` inside the same window — **10 chromium workers at 70–88% CPU starved the battery past 23 minutes** until it had to be abandoned and re-run guard-by-guard. **Lesson, general: "are the lanes idle?" is a cheap proxy for "is the machine quiet?", and the two answers diverge exactly when it matters most.** Ask `ps` for processes >15% CPU, not the lane table — and do not start a second battery inside a measurement window you opened yourself.

📋 **F-1643-2 CORRECTIVE AUTHORED + DISPATCHED s1646 (2026-08-11) — `tasks/lane-a-f1643-2-suite-red-inventory-refresh.md` → lane-a; goal leaf `f1643-2-suite-red-inventory-refresh` registered in the SAME commit.** **This is the first fire at which the row's own GATE was open** — all four lanes `ahead=0` USABLE and the fire's only drain (`57208ef8`) fully committed before authoring; s1644 and s1645 each declined for want of exactly that. 🔬 **THE HARNESS WAS PROVED BEFORE THE SCOPE WAS WRITTEN, because two facts neither row above records would have sunk a guessed master:** (1) **`logs/suite-red-inventory-raw.json` DOES NOT EXIST** — untracked (not gitignored; `git ls-files logs/` returns only the `.md` and the compact JSON) and the copy behind the 2026-07-28 snapshot is gone from disk, so the reducer's input must be **produced**, and running the reducer alone gives `ENOENT` rather than a stale-but-working report; (2) **the command that produced that snapshot is documented nowhere** — not in the `.md`, not in `scripts/`. s1646 therefore ran the whole pipeline on one cheap spec first (`PLAYWRIGHT_JSON_OUTPUT_NAME=… npx playwright test e2e/_s106-prospector-boot-probe.spec.ts --reporter=json` → 6,026-byte raw → `scripts/suite-red-inventory.mjs` → well-formed inventory), so scope 1–2 is a **proven sequence scaled up**, not a guess. ⚠️ **F-1646-3 — THE GATE'S LETTER IS NOT ITS PURPOSE, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS MEASURABLE RIGHT NOW.** The gate is written in terms of **lanes**, and lanes are not the only load on this machine: with all four idle, `ps` showed **Blender at 100% CPU for 2h26m** (`Blender -b assets/blender/fries-mcdx/landmark.blend`, a headless art-pipeline render) **plus `fseventsd` at 100%** — **2 of 16 physical cores saturated by work no lane instrument can see.** A fire checking only `lane-usable --all` would have called the machine quiet and been wrong, which is precisely why the gate's third clause (*"state the concurrent load it ran under"*) exists; scope 4 obeys it literally at run start **and** end. ⭐ **Scope 5 adds what no row asked for and what makes the refresh trustworthy rather than merely fresh:** every failing spec **file** is re-run **serially, once**, and any test that fails in parallel but passes serially is declared **load-attributable** instead of entering the inventory as an excused red — the exact outcome the RE-PRICED row warns is *"strictly worse than the 13-day-old one it replaced."* 🚧 The firewall forbids editing the suite being measured (*a measurement that edits its subject is not a measurement* — no fixing, skipping, quarantining or re-pinning a single failing test) and forbids patching either script (a defect in the reducer is a **FINDING and a STOP**, never a drive-by). Acceptance is one line: `red-inventory-lookup e2e/front-door-parity.spec.ts` must stop answering `NOT-IN-INVENTORY` + `STALE SNAPSHOT` (now **14 days / 378 commits**, re-measured s1646).

✅ **SHIPPED s1646 `57208ef8` — AP-16-5b: THE SECOND DOOR LEARNED THE FACING, AND STOPPED REFUSING THE ORDER OUTRIGHT.** Review `reviews/ap16-5b-seat-rotation-parity.md`; goal leaf `ap16-5b-seat-rotation-parity` → `merged`; done-move retired. `src/sim/SeatOrders.ts` — the multiplayer seat's build path — now accepts `rotationSteps: 0..3` and forwards it, closing F-1645-2. ⭐ **The value is in the failure mode it removed, not in parity bookkeeping:** because `parseSeatOrder` gates on `exactKeys`, the unknown field was never *ignored* — it was a schema error that rejected the **whole order**, so an agent obeying the grammar AP-16-5 had just published in `public/skill.md` got `INVALID_ARGS` and placed **nothing**. A hard refusal reachable from the factory's own documentation is strictly worse than a silent default. 🔑 **The ruling was COPIED, not re-decided** — read against `src/agent/StandingOrders.ts:411-422` the seat now mirrors it predicate for predicate (same `'rotationSteps' in value` probe, same conditional key list, same `Number.isInteger`/`0..3` rejection, same conditional spread, same `?? 0` at the execution site), so VALIDATE-not-NORMALIZE holds on both doors and no second, quieter grammar was minted. 🚧 **Scope 2 honoured exactly:** the `BUILD_KEYS` constant at `SeatOrders.ts:48` is **unchanged**; the widening is conditional at the call site, so strictness is untouched everywhere else the constant is used. 🎯 **CONTROL, because a green alone is not evidence the change is why:** reverting **only** `src/sim/SeatOrders.ts` to main in the same tree, same shell, same hour reds **1 of 6 — exactly the new test, no collateral — and it reds with the DEFECT ITSELF** (`INVALID_ARGS`, *"orders[0] does not match the BUILD schema"*, for a well-formed rotation order), reproducing F-1645-2 verbatim; restored byte-identically and verified **by blob hash** `e69fd44b`, not by `git status`, which per F-1295-1 cannot tell a revert from a concurrent sweep. 🦷 **Teeth taken independently of both the runner's report and the shipped suite:** `0/1/2/3` each reach the sim's `place_build` at that facing, omission reaches facing `0` (the compatibility claim), and `7`, `-1`, `1.5`, `'north'`, `null` **all** return `INVALID_ARGS` — the last three being inputs the shipped test does not cover. 🔗 The facing reaches a **real placed building**, not just an action object, traced end to end: `buildActions` → `SeatedLockstepSim.ts:330` → `HeadlessContractSim.ts:642` → `BuildSystem.confirmPlacement` (`BuildSystem.ts:1048`), which sets `ghostRotationSteps` and re-syncs the ghost before confirming — which is also why the master's FIREWALL LIFT went **unused and correctly so**. **GATES** (fire shell, `--workers=1`, detached worktree `gate-s1646` per §3.0b, merged+committed as ONE act per F-1589-5): tsc clean · build green · node-guards run **ALONE** = **447 tests / 442 pass / 0 fail / 5 skip, ZERO `gr-sim` drift** · own suite **5 → 6, 6/6** · `same-game-audit` 3/3 with **counts UNMOVED** (agent-lacks 439, equal 641) · `e2e/ap16-5-rotation-parity` **4/4** both projects · plain boot **2/2** desktop+390px, zero console/page errors. ⓘ **F-1646-1 filed, advisory, NOTHING OWED:** the shipped test pins the rejection ruling at `7` and `'north'` only, so its denominator is narrower than the ruling it defends; `-1`, `1.5` and `null` were each verified correct at this gate, making it a note for whoever next opens that file rather than a corrective worth a queue slot.

✅ **SHIPPED s1643 `d4fcc354` — ap16-4 SAME-GAME ADMISSION: THE DOOR STOPPED BEING A LIST AND BECAME A DERIVATION (12 contracts → 22).** Review `reviews/ap16-4-same-game-admission.md`; master `tasks/lane-a-ap16-4-same-game-admission.md` (FIRE-AUTHORED s1642, drained s1643). `SUPPORTED_CONTRACTS` is now `listBoardContracts()` minus browser-unofferable (`harvestAnchors: []`) minus a **cited** exemption table — `CONTRACT_ADMISSION_EXEMPTIONS`, 5 entries, whose `citation` field is TYPED ``F-${string}` | `reviews/${string}.md``, so an uncited exemption is a COMPILE ERROR. That is the spec’s *“an exemption is a debt, not a policy”* enforced by the compiler instead of by a reviewer’s memory. Reachability `equal 12 · divergence 30 · not-offered 0` → `equal 22 · divergence 5 · not-offered 15`; **F-1642-1’s predicted 15/15 split CONFIRMED by measurement**, all 10 genuine candidates admitted. 🔑 **THE ERA CLAIM WAS TESTED, NOT RESTATED (scope 8): `gr-sim` pins show ZERO hash drift, nothing re-pinned** — so the additive-law premise holds and no era re-stamp is owed. ⭐ **The tautology s1642 named IN ADVANCE was closed:** re-aiming `skillmd-guard` from a source-regex to the evaluated module is exactly how F-1636-1’s tautology was born, so the drain re-ran both the assertion AND its manufactured-defect positive control on the merged tree — both green, the guard reads a real value and still bites. **GATES** (fire shell, `--workers=1`, detached worktree `gate-s1643` per §3.0b, merged+committed as ONE act per F-1589-5): tsc clean · build green · admission+E2/E4 censuses **18/18** desktop+390px · `skillmd-door`+`ap-standing-orders` **14/14** unmodified-green incl. plain boot · node-guards **446 tests / 0 fail** in 3 serial batches. **Classification: 9 paths ALL LANE-ONLY**, main’s 16 moved paths intersect them in the EMPTY SET, no graft. ⚖️ **The owner’s gauntlet park (“lets wait until we have parity between humans and agents on the maps”) LIFTS on this merge** — the spec names this sli