ⓘ **F-1620-8 (s1620 2026-08-10, OBSERVED AT THE f1619-2 DRAIN — NON-BLOCKING): desktop and mobile disagree on the `saveData` delta by 37%, and the reason is sitting unread in the artifact.** Drain run: desktop delta **4,029,556** vs mobile **2,943,858**. Both arms exclude the same two bulk halls (**2,584,796** bytes), so the ~1.1 MB difference comes from the *other* thing `saveData` does — narrowing advance-stream prefetch to `priority === 1` — which evidently removes more on desktop than on mobile. **The per-URL tables contain the answer; nobody has read it out.** **GATE: none owed. Worth one pass before the delta is quoted anywhere.** ✅ **ANSWERED s1621 BY READING THE COMMITTED ARTIFACT — AND THE SIGN REVERSES, SO THE FINDING'S FRAMING IS REFUTED RATHER THAN CONFIRMED.** ✓ VERIFIED by reading `artifacts/asset-diet/town-budget-desktop-chrome.md` and `…-mobile-chrome.md` on main. ⚠️ **First, an identification the finding did not make: the committed artifact is the RUNNER's run, not the drain's** — its mobile normal reads `22,110,931`, which `reviews/f1619-2-asset-diet-town-budget-ab.md` names as the runner figure against the drain's `22,469,519`. So the drain's per-URL tables, which F-1620-8 quotes totals from, **were never committed and cannot be decomposed by anyone now.** 📐 **In the run that IS on disk, both deltas decompose EXACTLY, to the byte:** desktop `2,584,802` = the two bulk halls (`dynamo-hall` 1,407,064 + `stamp-mill` 1,177,732 = **2,584,796**) + 6 B of `.js` shim noise; mobile `2,910,139` = the same 2,584,796 + **eight `char-hero-sheet-walkdiag8-r2c*` PNG sprite sheets totalling 323,909 B** + 1,434 B of shim noise. Nothing is unaccounted for in either. ➡️ **THEREFORE THE ANSWER TO "what does the `priority === 1` narrowing remove?" IS: on mobile it removes eight hero walk-sheet PNGs (323,909 B); on desktop it removes NOTHING beyond the halls** — those PNGs do not appear in the desktop table at all, meaning both desktop arms treated them identically. ⚠️ **This is the OPPOSITE of the finding's premise.** F-1620-8 reasoned from `desktop 4,029,556 > mobile 2,943,858` that the narrowing "removes more on desktop than on mobile"; in the committed run **mobile is the larger delta** (`2,910,139 > 2,584,802`, by exactly the 325,337 B those PNGs and the shims account for). 🔎 **The reconciling fact, and it points at an instrument rather than a behaviour: mobile is STABLE across the two runs (2,910,139 vs 2,943,858, 1.2% apart) while DESKTOP swings 2,584,802 → 4,029,556, a 1.44 MB / 56% move on the same build.** 💡 **That is F-1620-3's instability, in a second column** — F-1620-3 already measured the desktop `Wire bytes` column run-to-run unstable by up to 3×. **So F-1620-8 is best read as one sample of an unstable desktop quantity, not as a desktop-vs-mobile behavioural difference, and the 37% figure must NOT be quoted as a platform fact.** ⚠️ **What is NOT established:** why desktop is the unstable side, and whether the drain-run desktop figure would decompose the same way — unanswerable without its per-URL table. **GATE REVISED: the "read the tables" duty is DISCHARGED. What remains is a stability question, which belongs to F-1620-3, not here — and the practical rule stands: quote neither delta as a platform difference until desktop is shown to reproduce.**

✅ **SHIPPED s1635 `c5687d42` — F-1620-6 (OWNER RULED **"B"** 2026-08-10 — cure slice `lane-b-minds-rigs-full-width` AUTHORED+QUEUED the same hour, attended; MERGED the same day; s1620 original: at DESKTOP width the new aggregate table scrolls horizontally while visibly free width sits unused beside it.** `reviews/shots-minds-and-rigs/rigs-desktop-chrome.png` at 1280px clips mid-column — `Declared cost` shows `90,000 in · 8,00`, the sub-line reads `1 declared · 0 undeclar`, the expanded cell reads `The Dry Gulc` — while the area beneath the Front Desk sidebar card is **empty**. ✓ **The containment is CORRECT and is not the defect**: the table scrolls in-container (`reader.css:312`/`:469` `overflow-x: auto`) and never scrolls the page, which is exactly what the master required and what the spec asserts. 💡 **The point is narrower and worth keeping: the requirement was written for 390px and the rule it produced applies at EVERY width** — so a constraint authored for the small viewport now costs columns on the large one. ⚠️ **It bites precisely where the owner aimed:** the stated audience is people *"interested in the details"*, and the details are the columns being clipped. **GATE: none owed to the owner. A follow-up may let the Field Book content column reclaim the sidebar's width below the Front Desk card, or drop lower-priority columns only at narrow widths.** 🔧 **MECHANISM ESTABLISHED s1621 — AND IT SHOWS THE FIRST OF THE TWO PROPOSED CURES IS NOT EXPRESSIBLE IN THE CURRENT LAYOUT, SO THIS IS AN OWNER CHOICE RATHER THAN A FIRE-AUTHORABLE TIDY-UP.** ✓ VERIFIED by reading `src/encyclopedia/reader.css` and by eye against `reviews/shots-minds-and-rigs/rigs-desktop-chrome.png`. **The cause is `.claim-ledger__board-layout`: `grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(300px, 340px)` with `align-items: start`.** `align-items: start` makes the Front Desk CARD only as tall as its content — which is why the area beneath it looks free — but **the sidebar COLUMN still reserves its 300–340px for the entire row**, because grid tracks are rectangular. ➡️ **So "let the content column reclaim the sidebar's width below the Front Desk card" cannot be done by adjusting this grid at all** — it is a float/exclusion layout, i.e. a rewrite of the board's column model, not a tweak. ⚠️ **That matters because the finding's GATE reads `none owed to the owner`, and on this evidence that under-states it:** the three realistic options have visibly different outcomes on a surface the owner commissioned by name, so picking between them is a look-and-feel call, not a correctness one. **(A) Narrow the reserved sidebar track** (e.g. `minmax(260px, 300px)`) — one line, gives the table ~40–80px, does not fix the clip, keeps the empty region. **(B) Let the table span BOTH tracks below the card** (sidebar becomes a full-width header strip, or the table moves to its own full-width row) — fully fixes the clip at 1280px, costs the side-by-side reading the current design chose. **(C) Drop or collapse lower-priority columns only at narrow widths** — fixes 390px, leaves desktop exactly as it is, and desktop is where this was observed, so **(C) does not address this finding.** 💡 **RECOMMENDATION: (B).** The owner's stated audience is people *"interested in the details"*, and (B) is the only option that actually stops clipping the details; the sidebar is four short links and a two-sentence instruction, which reads fine as a strip. ⓘ **NOT AUTHORED s1621, deliberately** — a fire that picks a layout for owner-commissioned work is inventing scope (CLAUDE.md §7.3). **GATE MET (owner 2026-08-10): the word was "B" — tables span full width below a Front Desk strip. Slice `tasks/lane-b-minds-rigs-full-width.md` queued lane-b, leaf `minds-rigs-full-width` registered; this row closes when it merges.** ✅ **CLOSED s1635 — MERGED `c5687d423931cd57118bb9314482f3ed1b58b784`, review `reviews/minds-rigs-full-width.md`.** The cure is a new `.claim-ledger__board-layout--field-book` modifier + one `@media (min-width: 561px)` block; **the clipped cell now reads `90,000 in · 8,000 out · 18 calls` WHOLE**, verified against my own regenerated screenshot on the merged tree, not the runner's. 🔑 **The (B)-vs-County-Standings worry this row raised did not need adjudicating: the two boards render from DIFFERENT functions** — `reader.ts:314` (`county-standings`) and `reader.ts:370` (`field-book`) — **so only the Field Book can receive the modifier, and County Standings' two-column layout is preserved structurally rather than by care** (verified by reading both sites; `lb-01-county-standings` green alongside). ✅ **CONTROL RUN, because a passing guard never executes its violation path:** reverting ONLY the two source files to main while keeping the new spec goes **RED at `field-book.spec.ts:292`** — `scrollWidth <= clientWidth` returns `false` — so the shipped assertion genuinely catches the old defect. ⚠️ **Stated honestly (F-1635-2): the SECOND assertion (`clientWidth >= 900`) is never reached, because the first throws first — it is NOT independently proven, and this row does not claim it is.** GATES: tsc rc=0 · build green · field-book **8/8** desktop+390px with zero console/page errors asserted in-spec at `:296`/`:316` inside the two **plain-boot** tests (Mistake #10) · adjacent `lb-01-county-standings`+`en-01-claim-ledger`+`078-ux-hygiene`+`board-era-chapters` **34/34** both projects · all `--workers=1` · gated in detached worktree `gate-s1635` (§3.0b) and merged as ONE act (F-1589-5).

⭐ **F-1620-2 (s1620 2026-08-10, MEASURED AT THE f1619-1 DRAIN — SUCCEEDS F-1616-3 AND CARRIES THE LIVE HALF OF ITS QUESTION. AN A/B THAT ELIMINATES THE SUSPECT YOU THOUGHT OF STILL INHERITS EVERY SUSPECT YOU DID NOT.)** ⚙️ f1619-1 removed `page.route` and the double-download survived — so the re-request is **not an interception artifact**, four runs over. ⚠️ **But `CACHE-HIT = 0`, the most persuasive number in the table, is FORCED BY THE HARNESS AND IS NOT EVIDENCE ABOUT THE PREFETCH AT ALL.** ✓ **VERIFIED at the drain by probing the server directly rather than by reading the runner report:** `playwright.config.ts:69` runs `npm run dev`, and that Vite server answers a GLB with `Cache-Control: no-cache`, a weak ETag `W/"7904068-1786323900779"`, and a conditional replay returning **`304`**. Under `no-cache` the browser **must revalidate before reusing any entry**, so a CACHE-HIT — a fetch served wholly from cache with no network trip — is **impossible by construction**. The zero was decided by the server before the prefetch was ever consulted. ➡️ **CONSEQUENCE: the defensible verdict is "NOT AN INTERCEPTION ARTIFACT", not "real property of play".** The deployed game is served by Cloudflare Pages with long-lived `immutable` headers on hashed assets — a materially different cache contract — and **that case is UNMEASURED**. 💡 **The reusable half, and the reason this is a finding rather than a footnote: the probe was correctly designed, correctly firewalled, and still could not see its own second confound, because the confound lives in the HARNESS rather than in the subject or the instrument.** F-1616-3 offered *artifact or real* as exhaustive alternatives and they were not. **GATE: none owed to the owner — this is an engineering question, not a design fork. Closes when the same five-door walk is measured against a preview/production-headers server (`vite preview` or the Pages URL) with `classify()` reading HTTP status per F-1620-1. Any fire may take it.**

🔧 **F-1620-1 (s1620 2026-08-10, VERIFIED BY READING THE CODE AT THE f1619-1 DRAIN — A CLASSIFIER THAT CAPTURES A FIELD AND NEVER READS IT REPORTS A CONFIDENT WRONG NUMBER, NOT AN ERROR.)** ⚙️ `e2e/advance-stream-cache-reuse.spec.ts` records `fetch.status = response.status` at `:68` — and `status` appears **nowhere else in the file**. `classify()` branches only on `servedFromCache || fromDiskCache || fromPrefetchCache`, then on `encodedDataLength > 0`. ✓ **A 304 Not Modified sets none of those cache flags and carries ~127 bytes of headers, so every revalidation scores as DOUBLE-DOWNLOAD** — which is exactly the `127` entries visible throughout the artifact. 📊 Splitting the bucket by transfer size: desktop **11 revalidation-sized + 18 genuine full re-transfers (~46.0 MB)**; mobile **15 + 14 (~42.3 MB)**. ➡️ **The defect is still large and still real — a five-door walk re-transfers ~46 MB it had already fetched — but the number is 18, NOT 29, and a follow-up must not pin a regression assertion on 29.** ⓘ **Non-blocking and deliberately not fixed at the drain:** the per-URL table carries the raw byte counts, so the artifact contains everything needed to recompute; the **label** is wrong, not the measurement. **GATE: none owed to the owner. Closes when `classify()` reads `status` (a 304 is definitionally a cache reuse) and the buckets are recomputed — fold it into the F-1620-2 production-headers probe rather than spending a slice on it alone.**

ⓘ **F-1620-3 (s1620 2026-08-10, MEASURED AT THE f1619-1 DRAIN — NON-BLOCKING): the `Wire bytes` column is run-to-run unstable by up to 3× and must never be cited as a bandwidth figure.** Same door, same build, same shell: town desktop **366,060,611 → 305,594,651**; town mobile **547,132,847 → 182,286,179**; town-return desktop **49,503,048 → 25,946,082**. ⚠️ **The bucket counts were IDENTICAL across those very same runs**, so the instability is in byte *attribution*, not in the walk. The column sums `encodedDataLength` over every fetch attributed to whichever door was current when `requestWillBeSent` fired, and the advance stream prefetches **across door boundaries** — so a long-running prefetch is charged to whichever door happened to be open when it started. **GATE: none owed. Any future slice quoting an MB figure computes it per-URL from the second table, which is sound.**

🔬 **F-1620-4 (s1620 2026-08-10, OBSERVED AT THE f1619-1 DRAIN — POSSIBLY LARGER THAN THE FINDING THE PROBE WAS WRITTEN TO SETTLE): the advance stream PREFETCHES the same URL two or three times, carrying the complete asset each time.** ⚙️ Visible throughout the artifact's `Prefetch bytes` column — `7904347<br>7904347` for `town-plate.glb`, `1219823<br>1219823` for `general-store.glb`, and on mobile a **three-deep** `7904347<br>7904347<br>7904347`. ✓ **The runner flagged this unprompted and correctly did NOT act on it** — `src/assets/AdvanceStream.ts` was firewalled BY NAME because a task that measures the prefetch path must not alter it. ⭐ **Note the sign: this is redundant traffic on the PREFETCH side, i.e. before any demand fetch is involved, so it is additive to F-1620-1's ~46 MB rather than an alternative explanation of it.** **GATE: none owed to the owner. Needs a cure slice against `AdvanceStream.ts`; sequence it AFTER F-1620-2 so the fix is measured against production headers rather than `no-cache`.**

