reader) reads as never-reported, and it is not: the county-board GAZETTE item published its player-facing outcome on 2026-08-08 under the LATER hash `964978bad` — *"Every entry now names the mind and the rig it declared"*. **The duty is to report a CHANGE; the check is keyed on HASHES**, and those are not the same thing whenever a feature arc spans merges. This is precisely the harm F-1600-1 named — *"a check that reports a discharged duty as owed reports it forever, and every fire pays the judgement cost again"* — surviving in a new place after that finding's own cure. ⓘ **NO further mechanism proposed, deliberately:** the only reliable discriminator is reading the item and the diff, which is what a judging fire already does. The cure is this row, so the next sweeper does not re-open `ba78dad5e` a third time. **GATE: none — sweep run, items written, nothing owed.** 📮 **WRITTEN: one GAZETTE + one ROUNDUP** in `marketing/outbox/gazette-queue.md`. Fort solidity (`219ba70d1`) was **broken out separately because it carries a LIVE OWNER CHOICE** — whether landmark forts may ever be damaged or besieged, with the wrecker's gap-flow bypass waiting on the same word — and the law says an owner choice is the one thing a roundup must never bury. The far ground (`f065907c7`) and the living paper (`16dff1dc5`) were batched into one ROUNDUP per the ≤3/week rule. 😳 **AND I COMMITTED THE VERY DEFECT I WAS WRITING ABOUT, WHICH IS WORTH ONE LINE BECAUSE THE NEXT FIRE TO DESCRIBE IT WILL BE AT THE SAME RISK.** Writing the phrase "a `\u0000` escaping fix" into this row wrote a **literal NUL byte** into `tasks/BACKLOG.md` — the exact fault `1a2871fd3` cured in `Game.ts`, and the reason that merge exists (a NUL makes `grep` treat the file as binary, so it reports **nothing**, not an error). It was caught within a minute only because my own `grep -n "escaping fix"` returned empty when it plainly should not have — **an unexplained negative result is a signal, not a nuisance** — and `scripts/nul-audit.mjs` then named it at offset 2921. Cured by constructing the NUL with `String.fromCharCode(0)` in a fix script rather than typing one (a file that types the character to fix the character re-introduces it); `nul-audit` now reads **CLEAN**. ⓘ The guard already existed and would have reddened at `test:ledger-guards`, my last act — so the mechanism worked; what nearly failed was noticing an hour earlier. ⚠️ **THEN IT HAPPENED A SECOND TIME, IN THE COMMIT WRITING THIS VERY WARNING** — quoting the offending phrase back into the row to explain it re-wrote the byte, and `nul-audit` caught it again within the minute. **That is the actual lesson, and it is not carelessness: any prose that QUOTES the character re-introduces it, so the hazard is a property of writing about NULs at all.** ➡️ Therefore: never paste the literal to describe it — write the six-character escape, and run `node scripts/nul-audit.mjs` after any ledger edit that so much as mentions delimiters.

