scripts/fire.md:23:E. **PIPELINE REFILL (owner directive 2026-07-06: "4–5 lanes working constantly")**: after drains, check every lane queue; for each EMPTY queue, `cp` the top unblocked item from `tasks/BACKLOG.md`'s ladder for that lane (respect its GATE lines; stale-check masters older than ~2 days against current main — refresh or flag, never blind-queue). **SALVAGE LIFECYCLE (owner UX ruling 2026-07-06 late): `save/<name>` = re-land pending (counts as waiting, keep visible); when its re-land MERGES, rename to `archive/<name>` in the same fire — archives never count as waiting and never rot anyone's experience. A save/* older than ~3h with a known conflict gets a RE-LAND ruling, not a hand-merge.** **LANE-SAFETY LAW (s61 forensics, 2026-07-06): NEVER refill a lane that has an undrained done-move or a lane branch ahead of main — lane pre-flights `reset --hard`, which DESTROYS unmerged predecessor output (w1-03 light-pass and polish-02 were lost exactly this way; w1-04 survived only as a reflog orphan). Drain the lane first, refill second. Any lane task you AUTHOR must use a drain-gated pre-flight: check the worktree/branch is clean-vs-main before resetting, else STOP and report.** ⚙️ **AND ASK IT WITH THE INSTRUMENT, NOT A FILE-LEVEL EYEBALL (F-1081-9, cured by mechanism s1272 `0db281e0`; the law surface wired up s1278, F-1278-1).** `node scripts/lane-freeze-classify.mjs <branch>` classifies EVERY path the lane’s ahead-commits touched into DUPLICATE / LANE-ONLY / MAIN-ONLY / BOTH-MOVED, **prints the denominator it actually looked at** (`paths=N`), and exits 0 only when the lane holds nothing main has not absorbed (exit 1 = HOLDS n, exit 2 = misuse — read from the code, s1278). ⚠️ **`BOTH-MOVED` is a triage bucket, NOT a loss verdict**: a file lands there whenever main moved at all, *including when main moved by absorbing the lane’s own hunk and adding more.* Ask the one-directional line-level question with `scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs`. s1271 froze three lanes for days on the coarse file-level read and **two of them were never at risk**, while the third held 140 lines main had never seen — a conservative verdict at the wrong resolution is not safety, it is a different way of being wrong. 🚦 **BUT THAT INSTRUMENT ANSWERS THE SAFETY QUESTION, NOT THE REFILL QUESTION — ASK BOTH (F-1298-4, mechanism landed s1299).** `lane-freeze-classify` asks *would a reset LOSE anything?*; a master's pre-flight asks *is this branch clean vs main?* and **STOPs on any unmerged commit, absorbed or not**. s1298 measured `lane/m4` LOSS-FREE, refilled it, and the runner burned **26,940 tokens for zero edits and zero tests** stopping at exactly that line — a lane can be **safe-to-reset and unusable at the same time**, and the fire had no instrument that said so. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BEFORE ANY REFILL: `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs <slot|branch>`** (or `--all` for the fleet). It resolves slot→branch from `git worktree list` itself (**lane slot names are NOT branch names** — ⚠️ **and the mapping ROTS: this parenthetical read "lane-a is `lane/m3`, lane-b `lane/m4`, lane-c `lane/e2-arsenal`, lane-d `lane/perf`" until s1464 measured it FALSE. `git worktree list` shows `worktrees/lane-a..d` checked out on `lane/a`, `lane/b`, `lane/c`, `lane/d`. All eight branches still EXIST, which is what makes this dangerous — the stale names resolve, they just answer about the wrong ref: `lane/m3` reads `ahead=5 behind=347` and `lane/m4` `ahead=7 behind=982`, so a fire measuring by this prose would conclude lanes a and b HOLD unmerged content and refuse to refill two lanes that are actually `ahead=0` USABLE — a false freeze. F-1464-3.** ➡️ **NEVER read the mapping out of this sentence — resolve it from `git worktree list`, which is what `lane-usable.mjs` does itself), and returns one word: **USABLE** (rc 0, `main..branch` empty + clean → refill freely) · **AHEAD-BUT-ABSORBED** (rc 1 — safe to reset but the pre-flight WILL stop; `--cure` archives the tip to `archive/<branch>-s<N>-absorbed-<sha>` **before** resetting, history first, then verifies `main..branch` is empty) · **HOLDS** (rc 2 — content main never absorbed: drain it or rule on the listed paths by hand; `--cure` REFUSES) · **DIRTY** / **BUSY** (rc 2). ⚠️ **Read the WORD, not just the code** — HOLDS and DIRTY and BUSY share rc 2 deliberately, because all three mean *don't refill* while naming three different owed acts. ⓘ It reports **tracked dirt and untracked debris separately and never merges them into one number**: `reset --hard` cures the first, `clean -fd` the second, and only tracked dirt can be someone's unsaved work. (The first draft of this very script printed two untracked `.pyc` files under the words "tracked dirt" — caught s1299 by running it on lane-a before trusting it.) **ART-SLOT LAW (F-1045-1 + F-1044-4, written into law s1053 — both findings ordered it and neither had ever been written down): (1) any fire that TOUCHES the ART slot — refill, drain, or raws processing — runs `node scripts/art-staging-audit.mjs` and reports **BOTH** its **AT RISK** count and its **LOCAL-ONLY** count in the handoff — those are the two buckets to read, and they name two different owed acts: AT RISK bytes are in no object database (**salvage them**), LOCAL-ONLY bytes are in git here but on no origin ref (**push them**). `--strict` exits 1 when either is non-zero. **Report them, do not merely trust them — this audit has now shipped a false zero TWICE, both times because its headline was narrower than the question printed above it. (F-1054-1, fixed s1054 `4051bf31`): it checked only whether a staging file's NAME was absent from main, so 36.54 MB whose name matched main but whose BYTES were in no object database read as `0 files`; it now classifies by blob hash. (F-1055-1, fixed s1055 `c8034384`): "in git" was `cat-file -e`, which asks only THIS disk — so the same 36.54 MB, sitting on a local-only salvage branch whose push had timed out, again read `AT RISK 0`; every in-git blob is now also checked against the remote-tracking refs. If you find a third such gap, fix the class, not the instance.** `worktrees/art/` is NOT a git worktree (no `.git`, absent from `git worktree list`), so its output is untracked-by-default and dies with the disk: F-1045-1 found **10 files / 16.5 MB** sitting in no commit, the oldest for **14 days**. That is a standing property of the slot, not one bad batch, and it is the RETENTION LAW's largest live hole. (2) Any ART refill must **cite the BACKLOG line proving the batch is unshipped** in the queued task or the handoff — art batches have been re-queued against already-shipped rosters (Mistake #8 shape).** If the ladder has nothing ready for an idle lane, **AUTHOR the next master yourself** (idle-fire authoring precedent: s41 m3-02, s42 m4-02) — but ONLY from an existing spec slice plus its evidence chain (predecessor's review file, audit verdicts, playtest findings — read them, quote them in the task). Follow the house task pattern (goal / scope / firewall / self-check, gates, sequencing laws), mark the header **FIRE-AUTHORED (attended review welcome)**, max one authored master per fire, no drain-budget cost — ⚖️ AMENDED (owner 2026-08-09, F-1511-5 ruling "Allow 2 masters when dry"): when the board is DRY (no drain existed this fire) and ≥2 lanes sit idle, the cap is TWO authored masters in that cycle; the cap protects quality, not idleness. HARD LIMIT — never invent scope: if the lane's next work has no spec slice, needs a design fork, or bends canon, do NOT author — flag **PIPELINE-DRY: <lane> (needs spec/owner)** in your handoff instead. Refills are non-drain actions (no budget cost). **MAIN THROTTLE (s72 lesson, 2026-07-06): while ≥3 undrained done-moves are waiting, do NOT refill the main queue beyond one small corrective — every main task holds the working tree dirty and blocks ALL lane merges. Drains outrank main throughput; re-queue paused main items (queue-paused/) once the pile is below 2.** 📮 **DISPATCH ORDER IS A LAW, NOT A HABIT — COMMIT THE MASTER AND ITS EVIDENCE FIRST, REFRESH THE LANE SECOND, `cp` THIRD, AND RUN THE MASTER'S OWN CITATION GREP BETWEEN 2 AND 3 (F-1424-3, measured s1424; folded in s1426 after being deferred one fire for a lane collision that has since cleared).** s1424 cured lane-c *before* committing the master, so the lane sat **exactly one commit behind** — the commit carrying the citation the master tells the runner to grep. The runner claimed the slot inside a minute and STOPPED: **44,007 tokens, zero files touched, zero work lost**, saved only because that master wired the grep as a hard STOP. ⚠️ **Curing the message could never have prevented this** — the fault is ordering, and no instrument was being asked about it. ➡️ **AND THE KEY ITSELF MUST BE PROVED BEFORE IT IS WRITTEN DOWN: grep the candidate span against the SOURCE FILE ON MAIN first — if it does not match *there*, it is not a key, it is a paraphrase (F-1425-2).** F-1310-1 says cite by CONTENT because coordinates rot; what neither clause said is that **`grep` is line-oriented and prose wraps**, so a key spanning a line break matches **nowhere, including in the file it was copied from**. s1425 caught this on its own dispatch: the key returned 0 in the lane and its first instinct was a stale lane, but `lane-usable` read `ahead=0 behind=0` and the same grep returned **0 against main too**. That failure is nastier than staleness because **the master specifies 0 to mean "the lane drifted"** — a false STOP whose error message accuses the wrong subject, sending the next fire chasing a phantom refresh. ⓘ **This is not a rare shape: s1426 hit it again while authoring, and all three of its first-draft keys returned 0 on main** — the fire had written the review file itself minutes earlier and its own prose still wrapped. Prefer a span that visibly sits on one line, verify **1 on main**, then verify **1 in the lane** after the refresh, and only then `cp`.
