scripts/fire.md:23:E. **PIPELINE REFILL (owner directive 2026-07-06: "4–5 lanes working constantly")**: after drains, check every lane queue; for each EMPTY queue, `cp` the top unblocked item from `tasks/BACKLOG.md`'s ladder for that lane (respect its GATE lines; stale-check masters older than ~2 days against current main — refresh or flag, never blind-queue). **SALVAGE LIFECYCLE (owner UX ruling 2026-07-06 late): `save/<name>` = re-land pending (counts as waiting, keep visible); when its re-land MERGES, rename to `archive/<name>` in the same fire — archives never count as waiting and never rot anyone's experience. A save/* older than ~3h with a known conflict gets a RE-LAND ruling, not a hand-merge.** **LANE-SAFETY LAW (s61 forensics, 2026-07-06): NEVER refill a lane that has an undrained done-move or a lane branch ahead of main — lane pre-flights `reset --hard`, which DESTROYS unmerged predecessor output (w1-03 light-pass and polish-02 were lost exactly this way; w1-04 survived only as a reflog orphan). Drain the lane first, refill second. Any lane task you AUTHOR must use a drain-gated pre-flight: check the worktree/branch is clean-vs-main before resetting, else STOP and report.** ⚙️ **AND ASK IT WITH THE INSTRUMENT, NOT A FILE-LEVEL EYEBALL (F-1081-9, cured by mechanism s1272 `0db281e0`; the law surface wired up s1278, F-1278-1).** `node scripts/lane-freeze-classify.mjs <branch>` classifies EVERY path the lane’s ahead-commits touched into DUPLICATE / LANE-ONLY / MAIN-ONLY / BOTH-MOVED, **prints the denominator it actually looked at** (`paths=N`), and exits 0 only when the lane holds nothing main has not absorbed (exit 1 = HOLDS n, exit 2 = misuse — read from the code, s1278). ⚠️ **`BOTH-MOVED` is a triage bucket, NOT a loss verdict**: a file lands there whenever main moved at all, *including when main moved by absorbing the lane’s own hunk and adding more.* Ask the one-directional line-level question with `scripts/lane-absorbed-lines.mjs`. s1271 froze three lanes for days on the coarse file-level read and **two of them were never at risk**, while the third held 140 lines main had never seen — a conservative verdict at the wrong resolution is not safety, it is a different way of being wrong. 🚦 **BUT THAT INSTRUMENT ANSWERS THE SAFETY QUESTION, NOT THE REFILL QUESTION — ASK BOTH (F-1298-4, mechanism landed s1299).** `lane-freeze-classify` asks *would a reset LOSE anything?*; a master's pre-flight asks *is this branch clean vs main?* and **STOPs on any unmerged commit, absorbed or not**. s1298 measured `lane/m4` LOSS-FREE, refilled it, and the runner burned **26,940 tokens for zero edits and zero tests** stopping at exactly that line — a lane can be **safe-to-reset and unusable at the same time**, and the fire had no instrument that said so. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BEFORE ANY REFILL: `node scripts/lane-usable.mjs <slot|branch>`** (or `--all` for the fleet). It resolves slot→branch from `git worktree list` itself (**lane slot names are NOT branch names** — ⚠️ **and the mapping ROTS: this parenthetical read "lane-a is `lane/m3`, lane-b `lane/m4`, lane-c `lane/e2-arsenal`, lane-d `lane/perf`" until s1464 measured it FALSE. `git worktree list` shows `worktrees/lane-a..d` checked out on `lane/a`, `lane/b`, `lane/c`, `lane/d`. All eight branches still EXIST, which is what makes this dangerous — the stale names resolve, they just answer about the wrong ref: `lane/m3` reads `ahead=5 behind=347` and `lane/m4` `ahead=7 behind=982`, so a fire measuring by this prose would conclude lanes a and b HOLD unmerged content and refuse to refill two lanes that are actually `ahead=0` USABLE — a false freeze. F-1464-3.** ➡️ **NEVER read the mapping out of this sentence — resolve it from `git worktree list`, which is what `lane-usable.mjs` does itself), and returns one word: **USABLE** (rc 0, `main..branch` empty + clean → refill freely) · **AHEAD-BUT-ABSORBED** (rc 1 — safe to reset but the pre-flight WILL stop; `--cure` archives the tip to `archive/<branch>-s<N>-absorbed-<sha>` **before** resetting, history first, then verifies `main..branch` is empty) · **HOLDS** (rc 2 — content main never absorbed: drain it or rule on the listed paths by hand; `--cure` REFUSES) · **DIRTY** / **BUSY** (rc 2). ⚠️ **Read the WORD, not just the code** — HOLDS and DIRTY and BUSY share rc 2 deliberately, because all three mean *don't refill* while naming three different owed acts. ⓘ It reports **tracked dirt and untracked debris separately and never merges them into one number**: `reset --hard` cures the first, `clean -fd` the second, and only tracked dirt can be someone's unsaved work. (The first draft of this very script printed two untracked `.pyc` files under the words "tracked dirt" — caught s1299 by running it on lane-a before trusting it.) **ART-SLOT LAW (F-1045-1 + F-1044-4, written into law s1053 — both findings ordered it and neither had ever been written down): (1) any fire that TOUCHES the ART slot — refill, drain, or raws processing — runs `node scripts/art-staging-audit.mjs` and reports **BOTH** its **AT RISK** count and its **LOCAL-ONLY** count in the handoff — those are the two buckets to read, and they name two different owed acts: AT RISK bytes are in no object database (**salvage them**), LOCAL-ONLY bytes are in git here but on no origin ref (**push them**). `--strict` exits 1 when either is non-zero. **Report them, do not merely trust them — this audit has now shipped a false zero TWICE, both times because its headline was narrower than the question printed above it. (F-1054-1, fixed s1054 `4051bf31`): it checked only whether a staging file's NAME was absent from main, so 36.54 MB whose name matched main but whose BYTES were in no object database read as `0 files`; it now classifies by blob hash. (F-1055-1, fixed s1055 `c8034384`): "in git" was `cat-file -e`, which asks only THIS disk — so the same 36.54 MB, sitting on a local-only salvage branch whose push had timed out, again read `AT RISK 0`; every in-git blob is now also checked against the remote-tracking refs. If you find a third such gap, fix the class, not the instance.** `worktrees/art/` is NOT a git worktree (no `.git`, absent from `git worktree list`), so its output is untracked-by-default and dies with the disk: F-1045-1 found **10 files / 16.5 MB** sitting in no commit, the oldest for **14 days**. That is a standing property of the slot, not one bad batch, and it is the RETENTION LAW's largest live hole. (2) Any ART refill must **cite the BACKLOG line proving the batch is unshipped** in the queued task or the handoff — art batches have been re-queued against already-shipped rosters (Mistake #8 shape).** If the ladder has nothing ready for an idle lane, **AUTHOR the next master yourself** (idle-fire authoring precedent: s41 m3-02, s42 m4-02) — but ONLY from an existing spec slice plus its evidence chain (predecessor's review file, audit verdicts, playtest findings — read them, quote them in the task). Follow the house task pattern (goal / scope / firewall / self-check, gates, sequencing laws), mark the header **FIRE-AUTHORED (attended review welcome)**, max one authored master per fire, no drain-budget cost — ⚖️ AMENDED (owner 2026-08-09, F-1511-5 ruling "Allow 2 masters when dry"): when the board is DRY (no drain existed this fire) and ≥2 lanes sit idle, the cap is TWO authored masters in that cycle; the cap protects quality, not idleness. HARD LIMIT — never invent scope: if the lane's next work has no spec slice, needs a design fork, or bends canon, do NOT author — flag **PIPELINE-DRY: <lane> (needs spec/owner)** in your handoff instead. Refills are non-drain actions (no budget cost). **MAIN THROTTLE (s72 lesson, 2026-07-06): while ≥3 undrained done-moves are waiting, do NOT refill the main queue beyond one small corrective — every main task holds the working tree dirty and blocks ALL lane merges. Drains outrank main throughput; re-queue paused main items (queue-paused/) once the pile is below 2.** 📮 **DISPATCH ORDER IS A LAW, NOT A HABIT — COMMIT THE MASTER AND ITS EVIDENCE FIRST, REFRESH THE LANE SECOND, `cp` THIRD, AND RUN THE MASTER'S OWN CITATION GREP BETWEEN 2 AND 3 (F-1424-3, measured s1424; folded in s1426 after being deferred one fire for a lane collision that has since cleared).** s1424 cured lane-c *before* committing the master, so the lane sat **exactly one commit behind** — the commit carrying the citation the master tells the runner to grep. The runner claimed the slot inside a minute and STOPPED: **44,007 tokens, zero files touched, zero work lost**, saved only because that master wired the grep as a hard STOP. ⚠️ **Curing the message could never have prevented this** — the fault is ordering, and no instrument was being asked about it. ➡️ **AND THE KEY ITSELF MUST BE PROVED BEFORE IT IS WRITTEN DOWN: grep the candidate span against the SOURCE FILE ON MAIN first — if it does not match *there*, it is not a key, it is a paraphrase (F-1425-2).** F-1310-1 says cite by CONTENT because coordinates rot; what neither clause said is that **`grep` is line-oriented and prose wraps**, so a key spanning a line break matches **nowhere, including in the file it was copied from**. s1425 caught this on its own dispatch: the key returned 0 in the lane and its first instinct was a stale lane, but `lane-usable` read `ahead=0 behind=0` and the same grep returned **0 against main too**. That failure is nastier than staleness because **the master specifies 0 to mean "the lane drifted"** — a false STOP whose error message accuses the wrong subject, sending the next fire chasing a phantom refresh. ⓘ **This is not a rare shape: s1426 hit it again while authoring, and all three of its first-draft keys returned 0 on main** — the fire had written the review file itself minutes earlier and its own prose still wrapped. Prefer a span that visibly sits on one line, verify **1 on main**, then verify **1 in the lane** after the refresh, and only then `cp`.
scripts/fire.md:28:**§3.0 IS IT ALLOWED? — THE FIRST COMMAND OF EVERY DRAIN, BEFORE CLASSIFICATION AND BEFORE YOU FORM AN OPINION (F-1104-7, guard shipped s1105):** run `node scripts/drain-block-check.mjs <done-move filename | taskfile | branch>`. **Exit 1 = STOP: do not drain, do not gate, do not "just check the merge."** Everything else in this section asks *is it READY* — and a policy block is **not a property of the tree**, so no git probe can ever see it. s1104 merged owner-gated rf-34 with `main..lane/m3` genuinely 1 ahead, a real two-dot diff, main never having moved the file, and a runner report it judged sound — then had to reverse it the same fire. **A well-argued runner report is not an unblock; it is often exactly what made the fork worth reserving for the owner.** The block lives in `tasks/goals.json` (`status:"blocked"` + `blockedReason`) keyed by `taskFile`, which every done-move filename already contains — so this is a lookup, not a judgement, and it costs one second. A block is lifted by the OWNER only, never by a green battery — 🏷️ **BUT READ `blockClass` BEFORE YOU CONCLUDE AN OWNER OWES YOU A WORD (F-1383-1, mechanism landed s1383).** `status:"blocked"` is OVERLOADED across two opposite refusals: **`owner-fork`** is the block this sentence describes, while **`gate-side`** is a FIRE-RECORDED readiness hold ("this has not been gated yet") that **no owner will ever lift** — carrying one to the desk parks it forever. **Measured s1383 across all 9 blocked leaves: 5 owner-fork, 3 gate-side, 1 `disputed` (its own reason asserts both).** ✅ **RE-MEASURED s1538: 6 blocked leaves — 5 owner-fork, 1 `disputed`, and `gate-side` is now ZERO (F-1538-2).** All three gate-side holds discharged the lawful way, by being gated and merged rather than by being re-labelled or dropped: the leaf s1383 named as the casualty of its own bug, `e1-authored-bundle-validation` — *"the cheapest real merge on the board"*, frozen because the label reached two of three leaves — is `status:"merged"` at `cbf0e143c043d82cc7eba8266a119f247e220718`, which is precisely the `cbf0e143` code-commit of the s1384 two-commit sequence recorded in the clause below. 💡 **The distinction earned its keep and then emptied its own bucket, which is the outcome to expect from a GOOD discriminator — so do NOT read `gate-side: 0` as evidence the class is obsolete.** It is a transient state by construction: any fire recording a readiness hold mints a new one, and the surviving 5 owner-fork + 1 disputed are exactly the residue that no green battery can lift. ⚠️ **Verify the fate before celebrating a shrinking census** — "gone from the blocked list" and "merged" are different facts, and only the second is good news; s1538 checked the mergeHash rather than the absence. The discriminator used to live ONLY in free prose, so it could be — and was — silently omitted: s1381's handoff records flipping "all three" of its leaves with an explicit *"GATE-SIDE HOLD, NOT AN OWNER GATE"* reason *"so the next fire cannot misread them as owner forks"*, and **the label reached TWO of the three**. The leaf it missed, `e1-authored-bundle-validation`, is the one **s1381 and s1382 each named as the #1 priority and "the cheapest real merge on the board"** — and this tool then asserted the owner-only reading on its behalf, freezing it. ⚠️ **The field changes NO exit code** — every class still exits 1 and still refuses the drain; it changes only who is told they can lift it. An undeclared or unknown class **fails safe to `owner-fork`**, so forgetting it can never unblock anything, and `scripts/block-class-guard.test.mjs` (in `test:ledger-guards`) reds if any blocked leaf omits it. 📐 **HOW THE "do not drain, do not gate" STOP ABOVE APPLIES PER CLASS, stated because s1383 introduced the distinction into a clause that predates it and must not leave it ambiguous.** For **`owner-fork`** the STOP is total and unchanged — do not gate, because gating an owner fork only builds a case toward merging the thing the owner reserved, which is precisely the rf-34 failure. For **`gate-side`** the STOP is **on the MERGE**: gathering evidence in a **detached scratch worktree** (§3.0b) is reversible, touches nothing, and IS the prescribed cure — but the merge becomes lawful only when the same fire **also flips the leaf out of `blocked` and records the evidence that satisfied the stated condition**. A green battery alone still lifts nothing; the leaf and the merge move together or not at all. 📌 **EXECUTE THAT AS TWO COMMITS IN ONE FIRE — the code commit IMMEDIATELY followed by the leaf commit — because the single-commit reading is jointly unsatisfiable (F-1384-1, filed s1384 by the first fire ever to perform this clause; re-verified s1385 by reading the guard).** This clause originally said the merge must land *"in a commit that also flips the leaf"*. But `scripts/goal-tracker.test.mjs:81` ("goal tree schema is valid") requires a `merged` leaf to carry a `mergeHash`, and `:80` requires that hash to match `/^[0-9a-f]{40}$/` — and the hash a drain records is the **main-side merge commit itself**. **A commit cannot contain its own hash.** ⓘ The two-commit sequence is not a concession, it is already the house practice and the guarded one: `7435fa7c`'s leaf update is a later commit, s1384 followed the same order (`cbf0e143` code → `992144d4` leaf), and the s1301 law below **requires** `test:ledger-guards` to run *after* the bookkeeping commit — which only makes sense if that commit is separate. ⚠️ What is forbidden is leaving the fire with the leaf still `blocked`; the *intent* — never leave a merged slice behind a stale block — is unchanged and binding. 💡 *A law written in the same fire as its cure has not yet been executed once; this clause was proved unexecutable one minute into its first real use.* For **`disputed`**, treat as `owner-fork` until an attended session rules. ⚠️ **UNKNOWN = no goal leaf matched: that is a Goal Registration Law bookkeeping finding, NOT a clearance — and you must read the WORD, because the exit code will not tell you (F-1262-3, doc corrected s1273).** This clause used to say "Exit 2/UNKNOWN", which is false by default: the no-leaf path ends in `process.exit(strict ? 2 : 0)` — `scripts/drain-block-check.mjs:431` (**was `:315` when written s1273, `:330` s1276–s1382, `:385` s1383–s1596; re-verified by READING the file and re-based s1597 after the F-1597-1 collector cure landed 46 lines above it — the FOURTH rot of this one coordinate, and the second running caused by a fire editing the very script the law cites** — cite the CODE, the coordinate drifts) — so an unmatched master exits **0**, identical to CLEAR. Re-measured s1276 on `003-vp-03-terrain-variety.md`: default **rc=0**, `--strict` **rc=2**, identical UNKNOWN text both times. A fire that branches on the exit code alone therefore reads UNKNOWN as permission — the precise failure §3.0 exists to prevent. Pass **`--strict`** if you want UNKNOWN to be rc=2, or parse the verdict text. 🚫 **The default is deliberately advisory and must NOT be flipped**: **538 of 941 masters (57.2%, RE-MEASURED s1538** — was `526 of 762 (69.0%)` s1276, and `~76%` before that**)** have no leaf, so a strict default would red the whole board rather than the one master you are asking about. 📉 **THE RATIO IS FALLING AND THAT IS THE MEASUREMENT'S REAL CONTENT, SO READ THE DIRECTION, NOT THE HEADLINE (F-1538-1).** Across s1276→s1538 the ABSOLUTE debt barely moved (526→538, **+12**) while the denominator grew by **179** — so **167 of 179 new masters (93.3%) were registered**, and the Goal Registration Law is demonstrably WORKING. The unregistered set is legacy debt, now decaying by dilution at roughly 4.5 points per 180 masters. ⚠️ **THE POLICY IS UNCHANGED AND MUST NOT BE FLIPPED ON THIS NEWS:** a 57.2% majority still lacks leaves, so a strict default still reds the board. **The number to watch is when this crosses below ~50%** — at that point the advisory-default argument stops being about "most of the board" and needs restating on its merits rather than on the count. Do not flip it early because the trend is pretty. `--all` audits every blocked leaf on the board. ⏳ **AND RUN IT ONE STEP EARLIER IN TIME THAN THIS CLAUSE SAYS — BEFORE YOU WRITE A NEXT-FIRE INSTRUCTION THAT ASSERTS A TASK'S CLOSURE STATE, NOT MERELY BEFORE YOU DRAIN IT (F-1669-1, measured s1669).** This section's trigger is *the drain*, so it never fires for the fire that has nothing to drain — and that is exactly the fire that writes the handoff telling its successor what to drain. **s1665 asserted that `f1643-2` was a `gate-side` hold, citing as its evidence the DONE-MOVE FILENAME `stopped-s1655-gate-side-…`; s1667 and s1668 each copied the assertion forward.** The block had been lifted by **s1657 `9a32d94f2`**, whose diff deletes `status:"blocked"`, deletes `blockClass:"gate-side"`, deletes `blockedReason`, writes `status:"queued"` and **dispatches the master** — 1h52m after that filename froze. 🪦 **A done-move filename is minted once at done-move time and is NEVER renamed: it is a fossil of a claim the ledger can reverse, and here it did.** Measured across `tasks/done/`: **6 of 1270 filenames carry a closure-class token, 5 resolve to a leaf, and 2 of those 5 (40%) are contradicted by their live leaf** — this one (`queued`), and `…s1176-blocked-by-26-cell-master-divergence-…-eight-winds-wiring-hero.md`, whose leaf reads `merged` at `29bac3d9478e9f3a77385c75965b60447a90500c`. ⚠️ **The cost is not a stale paragraph — it is an armed Silent No-Op (Mistake #1):** the stopped run that filename names **left zero diff** by its own preserved record, so a successor obeying the instruction gates a run that changed nothing, and looks for a `blocked` leaf to flip that does not exist. 🚫 **No mechanism is proposed and the reason is the point: the obvious one — have this tool print the filename-vs-leaf disagreement — is cheap and purely mechanical and STILL would not have prevented this, because s1665 never ran the tool.** It had the filename in hand and read its words. **A tool only helps the fire that asks it** (the F-1582-1 gap: the misread happens while a fire reads with its eyes, a moment no battery observes). ➡️ **The cure is therefore the one command you already have, invoked at the moment of ASSERTION rather than the moment of action — and it generalises: any name the factory mints to describe a state (done-move prefixes, `save/` vs `archive/`, row markers) is a SNAPSHOT of a claim, while the ledger IS the claim. On disagreement the ledger wins and the name is what must be re-read.**
scripts/fire.md:29:**§3.0b CUSTODY — NEVER PLACE CONTENT YOU HAVE NOT *DECIDED* TO MERGE INTO MAIN'S WORKING TREE. GATE UNDECIDED CONTENT IN A DETACHED WORKTREE (F-1295-1, s1295 — MADE STANDING s1342).** ⚠️ **This cure was declared "adopted" by exactly one fire and then written into NO law surface for 47 fires** — re-measured s1342 across `scripts/fire.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/skills/**`, `AGENTS.md` and every `^- **s9` bullet in `STATUS.md` (**140 total = 18 genuine lettered law bullets** — `s9i`/`s9ab`/`s9al`/`s9ar`/… — **plus 119 numeric handoff archives and 3 bare `- **s9 ` VM-era env bullets (obsolete on native, per the blanket VM-era clause)**; the loose phrase "140 law bullets" would over-state the law corpus 7.8×, so it is written out here — ✅ **RE-MEASURED s1538, 196 fires later: EXACTLY 140 / 18 / 119 / 3, every figure unchanged. DO NOT RE-DERIVE THIS ONE AGAIN.** It is stable *by construction*, which is the part worth knowing: the `^- **s9` pattern matches only the closed historical range s900–s999, so it cannot grow no matter how many fires run — today's archives are minted as `- **s15NN`. A figure that looks like a growing census is in fact a frozen one, and this line now says so): the only "detached worktree" in the corpus is `CLAUDE.md:50` (Mistake #12), which is about **contention**, a different question F-1295-1 itself explicitly distinguishes. §3's clean-main rule asks whether *someone else's* dirt contaminates your gate; **this asks the inverse — whether YOUR gate leaks.** 🎯 **THE INCIDENT:** s1294 checked four lane paths into main's tree to evaluate a slice, wrote **VERDICT: HOLD — NOT MERGED**, reverted with `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>`, confirmed `git status` clean, and was **wrong** — a concurrent attended session's broad `git add` had already committed all four at **`b37c1fc6`**, under a commit message about a rehearsal that named none of them, **thirteen minutes before the handoff denying it.** 🔑 **WHY THE VERIFICATION COULD NOT WORK, WHICH IS THE REUSABLE HALF: every probe that fire had returns the SAME answer whether the revert worked or the content was committed out from under it.** `git status` cannot detect a sweep, because **a sweep's signature is the ABSENCE of dirt**; and `git checkout HEAD -- <paths>` restores *from* a HEAD that has itself moved, so the "revert" **re-installed** the very content it was undoing. The only discriminating probe is a **blob-hash comparison against a PRE-sweep ref** — which is why F-1295-1 is recorded in hashes. ⚖️ **A HOLD verdict has NO ENFORCEMENT SURFACE:** it lives in a review file and a goal leaf, and **nothing in git defends it**. The same window ships an **owner-BLOCKED** slice just as readily — the version that costs a design fork, and the one §3.0 exists to prevent. **What must NOT be concluded is "the sweep turned out fine, so the sweep is fine":** that slice was exonerated by a control run *afterwards*, and had it gone the other way the defect would have shipped past a verdict that said no, with the fire that wrote the verdict still believing it held. Cost of the cure: one `git worktree add` plus a `node_modules` symlink. ⓘ **SCOPE, STATED HONESTLY SO THE NEXT READER DOES NOT OVER-CORRECT ME: F-1295-1 measured the WORKING-TREE case only.** Gating by `git merge --no-ff` and planning to revert on a red (§3, "revert the merge") puts undecided content on **HEAD** rather than in the working tree — structurally the same bet against concurrency, but **UNMEASURED as of s1342**; treat it as a caution, not a finding, and do not cite it as proven. ✅ **NO LONGER UNMEASURED — THE HEAD-SIDE CASE WAS MEASURED s1589 AND THE CAUTION IS NOW A FINDING (F-1589-5).** s1589 left drain 2's merge staged in main's index while writing its review; **ninety seconds later** a concurrent Cowork agent committed **`9371c1c8d`** — parents `e15858b13` (the fire's own stamp commit) and `153d30ba6` (the lane tip) — sweeping the entire staged merge into a commit headlined *"the operator falls at twin-banks w16 (697 kills) — notebook gen 2"*, which names none of it. ⓘ **Nothing was lost and nothing was corrupt** (`main..lane/b` empty, artifacts byte-identical, the fire's other drain untouched) — **the casualty was attribution, and the EXPOSURE is far worse than the outcome: had that gate gone RED, the plan was `merge --abort`, and the sweep would have SHIPPED the rejected content anyway.** That is the same failure as F-1295-1 with the staging area swapped for the index, and it retires the "structurally the same bet" hedge above: it is not merely structurally the same, it has now happened. ➡️ **THEREFORE, BINDING: never leave a merge staged on main.** Gate undecided content in the detached worktree exactly as this section already mandates, then **merge and commit as ONE act** — `git merge --no-ff <branch> -m "…"`, never `--no-commit` followed by review-writing. The review file, ledger rows and goal leaf land in a FOLLOWING commit; that is already the house sequence (§3.0's two-commit rule exists because a commit cannot contain its own hash), so this costs nothing and closes the window. ⚠️ **Do not read "the sweep turned out fine" as evidence the window is safe** — that is the exact reasoning this section already forbids one paragraph above, and s1589 is the second fire to be saved by luck rather than by design.
